<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Merged on top of the generated Info.plist (GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE stays
	     YES), the same arrangement ForgeWatch/Info-Add.plist already uses. Only
	     keys that cannot be expressed as an INFOPLIST_KEY_* build setting live
	     here — an array of dictionaries cannot. -->

	<!-- Lets iOS hand a CloudKit share straight to Forge when a supporter accepts
	     an invite, landing in userDidAcceptCloudKitShareWith. Without it the
	     share URL opens in Safari and stops there, which is the whole accept
	     path for partner sharing. -->
	<!-- Health usage strings also live as INFOPLIST_KEY_* build settings.
	     Duplicating them here means a missing build-setting merge cannot
	     ship an app that aborts on the first Allow tap. -->
	<key>NSHealthShareUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Forge reads Health app data such as activity, workouts, sleep, heart metrics, body measurements, cycle health, mobility, hydration, nutrition, and structured records to personalize training, recovery, and coaching.</string>
	<key>NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Forge saves workouts, nutrition, hydration, and energy data to the Health app so your fitness history stays complete and personalized.</string>
	<key>NSHealthClinicalHealthRecordsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Forge reads structured Health records — allergies, medications, conditions, immunizations, labs, procedures, and clinical vitals — so ARIA can coach around what is already on this iPhone. Names and dates only. Not notes. Not insurance. Nothing leaves this iPhone.</string>
	<key>NSHealthClinicalHealthRecordsShareUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Forge reads structured Health records — allergies, medications, conditions, immunizations, labs, procedures, and clinical vitals — so ARIA can coach around what is already on this iPhone. Names and dates only. Not notes. Not insurance. Nothing leaves this iPhone.</string>
	<key>NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Forge reads Calendar to see when you're busy so ARIA can fit training around your day, and may add labeled demo events to a Forge-owned calendar — never to your personal calendars. Titles and attendees stay on this iPhone and are never uploaded.</string>

	<key>CKSharingSupported</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Sleep soundscapes and the wake tone keep playing when the screen locks. -->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>audio</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Read at launch by ForgeAuthConfig.fromInfoDictionary.

	     Dummy-offline / TestFlight-safe. Empty apiBaseUrl (never a loopback
	     host) and empty Cognito ids are the offline signal, not forgotten
	     live config: cognitoConfigured stays false, so this binary does not
	     impersonate a Cognito build. Dummy ARIA needs no stack outputs and
	     does not turn Bedrock on.

	     Hex roadmap gates (FORGEProviderRoutingEnabled, FORGEEditableMemoryEnabled,
	     FORGERMSSDEnabled) stay absent here on purpose — off-by-absence. A
	     Dummy archive must not require live Cognito, a live API, an
	     ai_provider secret, or AWS stack outputs. The generator in check
	     mode refuses those keys if they are later added as true.

	     A live archive fills these from an applied stack with
	     scripts/generate_client_config.py, passing terraform output JSON and
	     the environment name (apiBaseUrl, cognito.region, cognito.iosClientId,
	     cognito.userPoolId). Dummy-offline is: scripts/generate_client_config.py
	     with the dummy-offline flag (zero stack outputs).

	     FORGEEnvironment is a control, not a label. dummy is the TestFlight
	     Dummy path. ForgeAuthPolicy refuses the tester override when it is
	     production-like, and ForgeAuthConfig treats any name it does not
	     recognise as non-production, the permissive side, which is why the
	     generator rejects a misspelling rather than writing it. The same
	     script in check mode enforces all of this in CI. -->
	<key>FORGEAPIBaseURL</key>
	<string></string>
	<key>FORGECognitoRegion</key>
	<string></string>
	<key>FORGECognitoClientId</key>
	<string></string>
	<key>FORGECognitoUserPoolId</key>
	<string></string>
	<key>FORGEEnvironment</key>
	<string>dummy</string>

	<!-- Used by the Messages extension to hand off to the app: forge://cycle/sharing
	     opens the sharing screen, which is where consent and revocation live. -->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.forge.ForgeSwift</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>forge</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
